window.docContents[150].resolve({"/Environment-and--___/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-CVC5-API--Environment-and--___--Memory-Safety":{"contents":"The cvc5 types for terms, sorts, solvers, etc. are all related to a notion of (term) manager\nresponsible for allocating and indexing terms and sorts. These types cannot interact and will\nproduce a runtime error if they do not agree on their manager.\n\nThis API abstracts away the term manager completely and enforces manager consistency statically\nusing a type-class called Ω. Virtually all types in this library have an implicit Ω argument\nthat can be though of as representing a specific term manager. On the sort Srt type for instance:\n\n#check Srt\n\n\nCvc.Srt [Ω] : Type\n\n\nΩ is an empty Prop type-class and will be erased at runtime; note that you cannot build an\ninstance yourself as the constructor is private.\n\nArgument of the Env monad representing a specific term manager.\n\nAs we will see next, Ω is tied to the cvc.lean environment Env and you need to use one of its\nrun-functions to actually run Env-code. The first problem this solves is that manipulating a\nvalue v tied to manager m after m has been freed is unsafe as freeing m frees v, so v\nshould only live as long as m is alive.\n\nSecond, as mentioned above, values tied to different managers should never interact as that would\ncause a runtime error. By encoding a specific term manager through an Ω-value, we can type-check\nthat sorts/terms/solvers/etc. are compatible by making sure they agree on their Ω-value.\n\nBinary conjunction.\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level CVC5 API\u0009Environment and  Ω","header":"1.1. Memory Safety","id":"/Environment-and--___/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-CVC5-API--Environment-and--___--Memory-Safety"}});